WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:10.180
A target makes nerve growth factor, called NGF, but too little for every sympathetic

2
00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:11.520
neuron that reaches it.

3
00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:13.960
So which neurons get to live?

4
00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.180
Share it evenly, and they all live or all die together.

5
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.120
If the first axon took everything, nothing would explain how a neuron receiving some

6
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:25.720
NGF could fall behind.

7
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.500
Something else must pick the winners.

8
00:00:29.483 --> 00:00:35.903
In mouse sympathetic neurons, NGF raises its own receptor, TrkA.

9
00:00:36.843 --> 00:00:42.163
With NGF, TrkA rises, and without it, TrkA falls.

10
00:00:43.083 --> 00:00:47.703
So a neuron with a little more TrkA collects more NGF.

11
00:00:48.183 --> 00:00:52.003
It makes still more TrkA and pulls further ahead.

12
00:00:52.763 --> 00:00:58.043
That loop is positive feedback, and it turns a small difference into a large one.

13
00:00:58.817 --> 00:01:05.777
In simulations with a fixed NGF supply, feedback on both TrkA level and signal

14
00:01:05.777 --> 00:01:09.197
duration split the neurons into winners and losers.

15
00:01:10.137 --> 00:01:13.817
A young neuron is naive and responds poorly to NGF.

16
00:01:14.757 --> 00:01:18.757
Enough NGF carries it over a threshold, which is sensitization.

17
00:01:19.337 --> 00:01:23.017
It raises TrkA, and that is a competitive advantage.

18
00:01:23.017 --> 00:01:28.077
Winning the supply is selection, and neighbours that stayed behind lose.

19
00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:31.590
Winners do more than pull ahead.

20
00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:36.990
Strongly supported neurons make BDNF and NT-4.

21
00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:41.730
These act through p75 on weakly supported neighbours.

22
00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:45.910
In this setting, p75 can promote death.

23
00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:53.650
A neuron rich in TrkA is protected, and a neuron poor in TrkA is not.

24
00:01:54.533 --> 00:01:56.213
Does punishment matter?

25
00:01:57.153 --> 00:02:02.693
In simulations, it made the split faster, because weak neurons lingered without it.

26
00:02:03.513 --> 00:02:08.793
In mice lacking p75, the loss is delayed but not prevented.

27
00:02:09.813 --> 00:02:13.473
Neuron number falls more slowly and ends at a similar level.

28
00:02:14.233 --> 00:02:17.653
The losers still die, because they lack enough NGF.

29
00:02:18.667 --> 00:02:22.687
So a limited supply does not pick the winners alone.

30
00:02:23.427 --> 00:02:28.187
A small early lead raises TrkA, and the lead grows.

31
00:02:29.687 --> 00:02:34.467
Winners punish weak neighbours through p75, and the split comes faster.

32
00:02:35.387 --> 00:02:41.247
All this comes from one cell type in one species, the mouse sympathetic neuron.

33
00:02:41.247 --> 00:02:48.567
Remember the rule: feedback turns a small lead into survival, and punishment speeds it up.
